<!-- ============================================================
     CrushFTP CVE-2025-54309 Unauthenticated Admin Bypass Detection Rules
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1190     - Exploit Public-Facing Application
       T1136     - Create Account (Local Account)
       T1059     - Command and Scripting Interpreter
       T1078     - Valid Accounts
     Rule IDs: 104054-104057
     Author: SecOps Engineer (ESKA-582)
     Date: 2026-07-20
     CVE: CVE-2025-54309
     CVSS: 9.0 (Critical)
     CISA KEV: Added July 2025 (federal deadline August 2025)

     Vulnerability summary:
       DMZ proxy AS2 protocol validation flaw in CrushFTP 10 < 10.8.5
       and CrushFTP 11 < 11.3.4_23 allows an unauthenticated remote
       attacker to obtain full administrative access. A single crafted
       HTTPS POST to the /AS2 endpoint bypasses authentication and
       returns an admin session token. Attackers use the resulting
       access to create a rogue admin account with a distinctive
       32-character hex username (pattern: [0-9a-f]{32}m) written
       to MainUsers/default/user.XML, then enumerate and exfiltrate
       files via CrushFTP protocols. Active exploitation confirmed
       July 18, 2026 (re-exploitation wave of the 2025 CVE).

     Detection approach:
       104054 - AS2 endpoint POST returning HTTP 200/302 in CrushFTP logs
       104055 - FIM alert: CrushFTP user.XML modified in MainUsers directory
       104056 - Shell or recon binary executed from CrushFTP working directory
       104057 - Authentication with 32-char hex username pattern (rogue admin)

     PRECAUTIONARY NOTE:
       These rules target CrushFTP application logs. If CrushFTP is not
       deployed in the environment, rules 104054 and 104057 will not
       produce alerts. Deploy them now so coverage is in place for any
       CrushFTP instance, present or future.
       Rule 104055 fires on Wazuh FIM events, which requires FIM monitoring
       configured for the CrushFTP installation directory (see below).

     Log source prerequisites:
       104054, 104057:
         CrushFTP access/event logs forwarded to Wazuh manager via localfile
         block. In syslog forwarding mode the program_name tag will appear
         as "CrushFTP" or "crushftp". Configure with log_format=syslog in
         the localfile block pointing to CrushFTP log directory.
       104055:
         Wazuh FIM (syscheck) monitoring the CrushFTP installation directory
         with realtime=yes and report_changes=yes. Add to ossec.conf:
           Linux:   /opt/CrushFTP*/MainUsers
           Windows: C:\CrushFTP*\MainUsers
         FIM base rules 550 (integrity changed) and 554 (file added) must
         be active (they are enabled by default in Wazuh).
       104056:
         Linux auditd with execve syscall auditing enabled (existing
         homelab auditd configuration covers this). CrushFTP runs as a
         Java process; child processes inherit the CrushFTP working directory.

     Severity tiers:
       High (12)    - Strong indicator, investigate promptly
       High (13)    - Near-certain malicious activity, investigate immediately
       Critical (14)- Confirmed rogue account creation, immediate response

     References:
       - NVD: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-54309
       - CrushFTP advisory: https://www.crushftp.com/crush10wiki/Wiki.jsp?page=Update
       - CISA KEV: CVE-2025-54309 (added July 2025)
       - Threat intel: detections/initial_access/crushftp_cve_2025_54309_unauth_admin_bypass.md
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="crushftp,initial_access,cve_2025_54309,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1190 - Exploit Public-Facing Application
       Rule 104054: AS2 endpoint POST returning success
       Log source: CrushFTP HTTP access log via syslog/localfile

       CrushFTP logs each HTTP transaction. Legitimate AS2 business
       traffic (EDI/B2B) is high-volume and originates from known
       partner IPs. A successful POST to /AS2 from an unknown source
       with no preceding partner session is the primary exploit signal.
       The bypass requires only a single request; any 200 or 302
       response to /AS2 from a non-partner source warrants triage.

       False positive sources: authorized AS2 trading partners sending
       EDI transactions. Tune by creating an allow-list rule chained
       to this one for known partner source IPs.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104054" level="13">
    <program_name type="pcre2">(?i)crushftp</program_name>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)POST\s+/as2[/ \?].*\s(200|302)\b</regex>
    <description>CrushFTP CVE-2025-54309: POST to AS2 endpoint returned HTTP success on $(hostname) - unauthenticated admin bypass exploitation (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>crushftp_as2_exploit,exploit_pubfacing,cve_2025_54309,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1136 - Create Account (Local Account)
       Rule 104055: FIM alert on CrushFTP user.XML in MainUsers
       Log source: Wazuh FIM (syscheck) - rules 550/554

       The rogue admin account is persisted by writing a new user
       entry to MainUsers/default/user.XML. This file is only
       legitimately modified by CrushFTP's own user management UI.
       Any FIM alert on a file whose path contains both "MainUsers"
       and "user.XML" is a high-confidence indicator of rogue account
       creation. The critical identifier is the 32-char hex username
       pattern [0-9a-f]{32}m that appears in the change diff.

       FIM prerequisite: syscheck block in ossec.conf must monitor
       the CrushFTP installation root with realtime=yes.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104055" level="14">
    <if_sid>550,554</if_sid>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)MainUsers.{0,200}user\.XML</regex>
    <description>CrushFTP CVE-2025-54309: FIM alert - user.XML modified in CrushFTP MainUsers directory on $(hostname) - rogue admin account creation post-exploitation (T1136)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>crushftp_user_xml,create_account,persistence,cve_2025_54309,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1059 - Command and Scripting Interpreter
       Rule 104056: Shell or recon binary executed from CrushFTP cwd
       Log source: Linux auditd execve records

       Post-exploitation: the attacker uses the admin session obtained
       via CVE-2025-54309 to invoke shell commands through CrushFTP's
       server-side scripting or event system. Child processes inherit
       the CrushFTP Java process working directory (typically the
       CrushFTP installation root). Shell and recon binaries executed
       with a CrushFTP directory as cwd have no legitimate operational
       justification in this deployment.

       Auditd prerequisite: execve syscall auditing must be enabled
       (existing homelab auditd ruleset satisfies this requirement).
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104056" level="12">
    <decoded_as>auditd</decoded_as>
    <field name="full_log" type="pcre2">(?i)cwd=\S*crushftp</field>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">(?i)/(bash|sh|dash|python3?|perl|ruby|curl|wget|nc|ncat|socat|whoami|id|uname|cat|find)</field>
    <description>CrushFTP CVE-2025-54309: shell or recon binary $(audit.exe) executed from CrushFTP working directory on $(hostname) - post-exploitation command execution (T1059)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>crushftp_child_process,execution,cve_2025_54309,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1078 - Valid Accounts
       Rule 104057: Authentication using 32-char hex username pattern
       Log source: CrushFTP auth/event log via syslog/localfile

       After creating the rogue admin account, attackers authenticate
       with it for persistent access. The account username follows a
       distinctive pattern: exactly 32 lowercase hex characters
       followed by the letter "m" (e.g. 5f3d8a1c2e4b6789abcdef0123456789m).
       This pattern is not generated by CrushFTP's normal provisioning
       flow. Any auth event matching this pattern is a confirmed
       post-exploitation indicator regardless of whether the initial
       exploit was observed.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104057" level="13">
    <program_name type="pcre2">(?i)crushftp</program_name>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(user|login|authenticated|account)[=:\s]+[0-9a-f]{32}m\b</regex>
    <description>CrushFTP CVE-2025-54309: authentication using 32-char hex username (rogue admin pattern) on $(hostname) - confirmed post-exploitation persistence via created account (T1078)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
      <id>T1136</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>crushftp_hex_username,valid_accounts,persistence,cve_2025_54309,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
